# Story 7.1: the Containerfile's builder stage COPYs the full repo checkout
# (stations locate their repo root by marker-file walk-up, so a partial
# checkout breaks that) -- this file only strips VCS metadata, local caches,
# and build artifacts that are never needed inside the image.
#
# PATTERN SEMANTICS -- .dockerignore is NOT .gitignore. Docker matches each
# pattern against the whole context-relative path, so a bare `__pycache__/`
# matches ONLY a root-level one and silently misses all 67 nested under
# src/**. Anything that can occur at any depth therefore needs an explicit
# `**/` prefix. Entries WITHOUT one are root-anchored ON PURPOSE, and each
# such case was checked against `git ls-files` -- a root-anchored rule that
# guesses wrong ships the thing it meant to strip (`.idea/` did: this repo
# tracks `recipes/.idea/`, 10 files, which the root-anchored rule missed
# until it was widened below).
#
# TRACKED FILES ARE THE HAZARD, not untracked ones. A rule that swallows a
# git-tracked file makes the image quietly unlike the checkout, and nothing
# reports it -- the same failure class `.gitignore` hit on 2026-08-09, where
# a blanket `*.log` emptied `tests/fixtures/error_logs/` and turned the
# failure-analyzer's two unmatched-log cases into false greens. `.gitignore`
# fixed that with a narrow negation rather than by weakening `*.log`; all four
# negations below are that same fix, one per broad rule that would otherwise
# take a tracked file (`.pixi/config.toml`, `.bmad-loop/bmad_loop_hook.py`,
# the two `conf/local/.gitkeep`s, `error_logs/unmatched.log`). Audit any new
# broad rule with: git ls-files | <match against this file>.
#
# UNTRACKED FILES ARE THE OTHER HALF, and only for one class: anything
# secret-shaped. The tracked-file audit above cannot see them by
# construction, so the secrets block below is maintained by cross-checking
# .gitignore's own secret-shaped entries -- see `conf/local/` there, which is
# exactly the case a tracked-file-only audit misses.

.git/

# The root `.pixi/` env is multi-GB and the builder stage materializes its
# own -- but `.pixi/config.toml` is GIT-TRACKED and load-bearing: it carries
# `run-post-link-scripts = "insecure"`, without which `pixi install` skips
# the post-link scripts that gdk-pixbuf (loaders.cache), gtk3, librsvg and
# graphviz all ship. Stripping it makes the image's install silently unlike
# every host install (verified: the host's pyforge-container env has
# `lib/gdk-pixbuf-2.0/2.10.0/loaders.cache`; a post-link-less install does
# not). `.pixi/*` rather than `.pixi/` so Docker drops each child wholesale
# instead of walking the whole env to honour the exception.
.pixi/*
!.pixi/config.toml
# Nested pixi workspaces materialize their own envs (recipes/insightforge/
# and each src/shared/packages/* member declares its own manifest).
recipes/*/.pixi/
src/**/.pixi/

# BMAD Tier-3 runtime scratch -- gitignored, local-only, never git-tracked.
# `_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts` is only a SYMLINK into the active
# project; the real per-project trees (run state, transcripts, patches,
# logs) live under projects/*/ and need their own pattern, or they ride
# along on any checkout where more than one station has run.
_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/
_bmad-output/projects/*/implementation-artifacts
_bmad-output/projects/*/_root-fallback-fork-*

# bmad-loop run state -- a loop home is itself a full repo checkout (this
# story was developed in one), so `docker build .` from a loop home would
# otherwise ship every run's transcripts, patches and logs: 11GB in the
# steward home at the time of writing, and agent transcripts are exactly the
# kind of thing that carries a pasted credential. This is BROADER than
# .gitignore's four `.bmad-loop/*` entries (`runs/`, `cache/`, `policy.toml`,
# `skill-projection.json`), which are narrow precisely so that
# `bmad_loop_hook.py` stays TRACKED -- so the blanket rule needs the negation
# below, or it swallows a tracked file and trips this file's own hazard.
.bmad-loop/
!.bmad-loop/bmad_loop_hook.py

# Build-host BMAD identity -- which project the marker/symlink point at is a
# property of the machine that built the image, not of the image. Baking it
# in makes two hosts produce two different images from one commit.
_bmad-output/planning-artifacts
_bmad/custom/.active-project
_bmad/config.user.toml
_bmad/_memory/

# Deprecated/historical content, not needed at runtime.
archive/

# Herald deck output -- large (84MB in this checkout) and not needed to run
# any station CLI. NOTE the real trade-off: herald's deck duty IS rooted
# here (`deck_pipeline.py`: `repo_root / "presentations" / slug`), so
# `herald deck` subcommands are host-only in this image by design. Story
# 7.4's volume mount is the seam if they ever need to run in-container.
presentations/

# Local secrets/credentials/state -- must never enter an image layer (these
# mirror entries .gitignore already treats as secret- or host-state-shaped:
# .env, .env.github, .secrets, .private, SDKs/). `.steward/` is the steward
# station's own repo-root state dir -- keys-inventory.yaml carries
# identity_path pointers, provenance and rotation history, budget.yaml the
# spend ceilings; neither belongs to whoever pulls the image. `/.herald/` is
# its exact analogue (AD-5's repo-local bridge/etag store, root-anchored in
# .gitignore for the same reason). `.claude/data/` is the gitignored mutable
# runtime cache (cf_atlas.db, vdb/, cve/, mapping caches) that Story 7.4
# mounts as a volume -- baking today's snapshot in would both leak whatever
# is locally cached and freeze stale state; `.claude/skills/data/` is its
# skill-scoped sibling.
#
# `conf/local/` is Kedro's canonical local-credentials path, and it is the one
# rule here that guards a file which ACTUALLY EXISTS in a normal checkout:
# `src/shared/packages/pyforge-atlas/tests/orchestration/conftest.py`
# auto-generates `conf/local/credentials.yml` (keys `bigquery_adc`,
# `github_token`) on any checkout that has run atlas's orchestration gates,
# and its own header says "Replace with real values for live runs". Atlas's
# `.gitignore` declares the path secret TWICE (`conf/local/**` and
# `conf/**/*credentials*`); without a counterpart here the stub -- or a
# maintainer's real token, once they follow that instruction -- lands in a
# shareable image layer with nothing reporting it. `**/` because there are
# three `conf/` trees (root, pyforge-atlas, pyforge-atlas-kedro-viz) and two
# of them have a `local/`. The `.gitkeep` negations are mandatory: both are
# git-tracked.
.env
.env.github
.secrets
.private
SDKs/
.steward/
.herald/
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/data/
.claude/skills/data/
**/conf/local/*
!**/conf/local/.gitkeep
**/conf/**/*credentials*

# Python build/test artifacts -- all occur at any depth (each package under
# src/shared/packages/ grows its own), hence `**/`. Every tool listed here
# is installed by this workspace's pixi features, so every one of these can
# exist in a developer's checkout.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info/
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.import_linter_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.hypothesis/
**/htmlcov/
**/.coverage
**/.venv/
**/build/
**/dist/
**/dist-conda/
**/build_artifacts/

# Atlas run-admission locks -- `data.locks/*.lock` + `*.holder.json`, written
# by `admission.py` on every `kedro run` and gitignored (atlas's .gitignore
# line 32; its own test says "Gitignored, but not hermetic"). Same reasoning
# as `.claude/data/` above: baking today's snapshot in freezes whatever the
# build host happened to be running -- an image built while atlas's tests
# were running ships ~90 of them, each recording a build-host pid and run id.
# Harmless by luck rather than design (exclusion is a live `flock`, which no
# image can carry, and a dead holder is reclaimed), but the image should not
# be a function of what was running on the machine that built it.
**/data.locks/

# conda/rattler-build leavings -- this is a packaging factory, so a checkout
# that has run a local recipe build carries these (none are git-tracked).
conda-bld/
miniforge3/
**/*.conda
**/*.tar.bz2

# Node artifacts (present under some presentations/* subprojects)
**/node_modules/

# Test suites -- Story 7.3 ("Credentials never enter image layers"). Never
# needed to run a station CLI, and shipping them is worse than dead weight:
# a REPO-WIDE unrestricted grep for `_SECRET_PATTERNS`'s three shapes
# (sk-ant-/AGE-SECRET-KEY-1/PEM-header) -- not scoped to any one package --
# found matches in exactly 3 files, all inside pyforge-steward's own
# `tests/` tree, confirmed by `steward keys audit --secrets
# src/shared/packages/pyforge-steward` (7 findings, same 3 files):
# deliberate synthetic fixtures proving the plaintext-secret scanner works
# (`tests/conformance/test_keys_plaintext_secret_scan.py`,
# `tests/conformance/fixtures/plaintext_secret_candidate/leaked_key.txt`,
# `tests/meta/test_invariants.py`), each word-marked (SYNTHETIC/TEST/
# PLACEHOLDER/NOTREAL), never a real leak. Stripping `tests/` closes that
# false positive at the source instead of teaching `scripts/container-gates`
# (or the shared scanner -- see DW-1-3-13) an allowlist. Live-verified this
# rule doesn't regress Story 7.1's own AC: a real `docker build` +
# `docker run <img> <cli> --version` for all 8 station CLIs (marshal,
# steward, pyforge-atlas, warden, doctor, mason, herald, scribe) still pass
# after this exclusion. `**/` because every
# package under src/shared/packages/* grows its own `tests/` directory.
**/tests/

# src/platform/ (Story 10.3, "One image, both engines") -- dev-only content
# under the platform's own tree that `src/platform/Containerfile`'s runtime
# stage would otherwise ship via its `COPY src/platform/ /app/` line.
# `**/tests/` (above) and `.git/`/`.pixi/` (also above) already cover that
# Containerfile's other exclusion needs -- this section is the REMAINING
# platform-specific gap. NOTE: this governs the platform build too, even
# though it lives in the repo-root ignore file rather than a
# `src/platform/.dockerignore` -- see the Containerfile's own top-of-file
# comment for why: its build context is the repo root (required so the
# builder stage can reach the workspace's `pixi.toml`/`pixi.lock`), so the
# repo-root `.dockerignore` is the one Docker/Podman actually consult; a
# separate `src/platform/.dockerignore` would sit there unused and mislead
# a future reader into thinking it's what governs the build.
src/platform/requirements/local.txt
src/platform/docs/
src/platform/utility/
# DEBUG=True + a fallback SECRET_KEY default (config/settings/local.py) and
# the test-only settings module (config/settings/test.py) have no business
# in a "production" image -- DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE is fixed to
# `config.settings.production` by the Containerfile's own `ENV`, so neither
# is ever imported at runtime, but shipping dev-only config as inert files
# is still the same class of hygiene gap this section exists to close.
src/platform/config/settings/local.py
src/platform/config/settings/test.py
# The root `.env`/`.secrets`/`.private` patterns above (this file's
# documented root-anchored convention) don't reach `src/platform/.env` --
# django-environ's natural local-secrets location for this app. No such
# file exists in this repo today (checked), but this Containerfile is the
# first thing in the repo to actually build a runtime image FROM
# `src/platform/`, so the gap stops being theoretical the moment one does.
src/platform/.env
src/platform/.env.*
# Build-host-generated app output. Both are in `src/platform/.gitignore`
# (`staticfiles/`, `platform/media/`) so they never reach a commit -- but
# .dockerignore is a SEPARATE list, and `COPY src/platform/ /app/` consults
# only this file. A developer who ran `manage.py collectstatic` locally
# would otherwise ship their host's assets into the image, where the
# runtime stage's own three-pass collectstatic runs over (never `--clear`s)
# them -- making image content depend on which machine built it. The image
# generates both directories itself; neither is ever an input.
src/platform/staticfiles/
src/platform/platformapp/media/

# Editor/IDE metadata -- `**/` because `recipes/.idea/` is tracked too.
**/.idea/
**/.vscode/
**/.junie/

# Misc local noise. The `*.log` negation mirrors .gitignore's: the
# failure-analyzer's fixture tree holds the repo's only tracked `.log`, and
# its absence turns two tests into false greens rather than failures.
**/.DS_Store
**/*.log
!.claude/skills/conda-forge-expert/tests/fixtures/error_logs/**/*.log
